ggml-org/llama.cpp#28671 ("CUDA: use radix-select TOP_K in the CUB fallback
for wide rows"), head ff2b436762b3, OPEN and unchanged since 2026-09-09.

WHY IT APPLIES TO THIS BOX, checked in the deployed binary rather than
inferred from the CUDA version: llama.cpp only gets cub::DeviceTopK from
CCCL >= 3.2, which ships with CUDA 13. This engine links cuda12.9, so
libggml-cuda.so carries DeviceSegmentedRadixSort (3 hits) and no DeviceTopK
(0) — i.e. the slow path, which sorts every key of every row and copies the
first k. The qwen4exp QSA indexer runs TOP_K over 4 rows x n_kv with
k = 2051, so at depth that sort is the dominant per-token GPU cost; the PR
measures 5.1ms -> 0.25ms per generated token at 131k on this same model and
quant, and +13-18% decode.

It engages only at ncols >= 8192, so it is inert for short prompts and this
box should see it only at depth — which is where qwen38-125b-a6b-max is
weakest (23.68 tok/s at 253344 tokens against 31.21 at 31538).

TIES BREAK DIFFERENTLY, and that is why this one cannot be promoted the way
#28213 was. The segmented sort always kept the lower index, so its output
was byte-reproducible; radix-select keeps whichever of two equal keys it
finds, like DeviceTopK. So "produces identical tokens" is NOT an available
acceptance test here. GGML_CUDA_TOPK_ARGSORT=1 forces the old path inside
the same binary, which is what the A/B should use.

Touches one file and cannot collide with the #28213 gather patch, which
touches src/llama-graph.cpp, src/models/models.h and src/models/qwen4exp.cpp.

MEASURED 2026-09-18 AND IT BUYS NOTHING HERE. A/B on a quiet box, warm page
cache, nCpuMoe 38, q8_0 KV, -ub 1024, identical prompts, one load each:

                 31538 tok            253344 tok
  control        456.6 pp / 31.21 tg  358.7 pp / 23.68 tg
  + this patch   438.7 pp / 31.40 tg  361.6 pp / 23.56 tg

+0.6% and -0.5% of generation. The PR reports +13-18% decode at depth on
this same model and quant, so the gap is the box rather than the patch.

THE LIKELY REASON IS THAT GPU TIME IS NOT THIS ENTRY'S CRITICAL PATH. At
nCpuMoe 38, thirty-eight of forty-eight MoE layers stream out of host RAM
every token; the overlay already said as much when #28213 came in under its
own headline number ("generation here is bound by streaming experts out of
host RAM, not by GPU attention"). Shaving milliseconds off a kernel that
overlaps with that streaming moves nothing.

WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT is the kill switch: GGML_CUDA_TOPK_ARGSORT=1 forces the
old path in this same binary. If that reads the same 23.6 tok/s then the
radix path is not engaging at all and the numbers above say nothing about
the patch; if it reads slower, the patch works and this box cannot use it.
Nobody has run that yet. It matters beyond this patch, because #28699 and
the rest of the qwen4exp GPU-side work are the same bet.

